#: ../../source/docs/common-3dr-radio-advanced-configuration-and-technical-information.rst:95
#: 0631f87d4857407e86785872e4b46fb2
msgid "the amount of signal being received on the ground"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-3dr-radio-advanced-configuration-and-technical-information.rst:96
#: 050c7f382068486ab954033e4624a158
msgid "the amount of signal being received in the aircraft"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-3dr-radio-advanced-configuration-and-technical-information.rst:97
#: 0e6e510d5e3c45e3a0fed330024a21d6
msgid "the amount of noise being received on the ground"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-3dr-radio-advanced-configuration-and-technical-information.rst:98
#: cfd7cc0ca5314993aac1d994b3b778b6
msgid "the amount of noise being received in the aircraft"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-3dr-radio-advanced-configuration-and-technical-information.rst:100
#: f9904abcea9549418e7ddb7d580b0b29
msgid "For the best possible range you want the two noise lines to be low, and the two signal lines to be high. In the above graph (taken from my SkyFun with a pair of 3DR 433 radios) you can see that the noise levels in the plane are higher than the noise levels on the ground. Also note that at the start of the flight (before I started the motor) the noise levels on the plane were lower, then they went up after I started the motor. That shows I'm getting some noise from my motor. If I wanted more range I would need to move the radio further from the motor and ESC."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-3dr-radio-advanced-configuration-and-technical-information.rst:109
#: 958c38ede31d434c90034a82c7221702
msgid "Perhaps the most common source of noise with the 3DR-433 is noise from the USB bus on your ground station. That shows up as high values for the RADIO.noise value. If you get this, then you could try using a different USB cable, or a different laptop. You can also try using a USB hub between your laptop and your radio."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-3dr-radio-advanced-configuration-and-technical-information.rst:115
#: be063c04d6ba4eddabcc307a441f09da
msgid "If the 'rssi' and 'noise' levels meet on the graph then you will lose the link. To determine what your range would be, a rough rule of thumb is to subtract the 'rssi' and 'noise' numbers, then divide by 2. That tells you your \"fade margin\" in decibels. For each 6dB of fade margin your range doubles. So if you have 18dB of fade margin, then you will be able to do roughly 8x whatever range you were at when you measured the margin."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-3dr-radio-advanced-configuration-and-technical-information.rst:123
#: a6cfdf3a163348f8a95d4f6ecc7fecd2
msgid "Another key source of range problems is the antenna placement. Your ground station antenna should be well clear of obstructions and a couple of meters off the ground. You may need to build a stand to hold it to get the best range."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-3dr-radio-advanced-configuration-and-technical-information.rst:129
#: 65334f3d79a347f8a41f840e4d97fe9c
msgid "Serial and air rates 'one byte form'"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-3dr-radio-advanced-configuration-and-technical-information.rst:131
#: 7cb1a52a41c74e529524f72776fe9b5a
msgid "The ``SERIAL_SPEED`` and ``AIR_SPEED`` parameters are in the same form that ArduPilot uses for the ``SERIAL3_SPEED`` parameter. If the number is greater than 2000, it is the baud rate.  Otherwise, it is the rate in kbps, truncated to an integer. So '9' means 9600 baud, '38' means 38400, '115' means 115200 etc."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-3dr-radio-advanced-configuration-and-technical-information.rst:138
#: cc130133b50b46288db71686e84ccb13
msgid "Choosing the air data rate"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-3dr-radio-advanced-configuration-and-technical-information.rst:140
#: 1187dced0eb14a6f90a2f777e3cab4a4
msgid "The key parameter that controls the range of your radios is the ``AIR_SPEED``. The default is 64 (which is 64kbps) will give you a range of over a kilometre with small omni antennas. The lower you set the ``AIR_SPEED`` the longer your range, although lowering the ``AIR_SPEED`` also lowers how much data you can send over the link."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-3dr-radio-advanced-configuration-and-technical-information.rst:146
#: 34796461683a44c6962ca08d72ceaf93
msgid "The radio firmware can only support 13 possible air data rates, which are 2, 4, 8, 16, 19, 24, 32, 48, 64, 96, 128, 192 and 250. If your application needs a different air rate for some reason then we can potentially add it to the register tables. If you choose an unsupported air rate then the next highest rate from the supported list will be chosen."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-3dr-radio-advanced-configuration-and-technical-information.rst:153
#: a4ebde980bed4a1dbc494226c7ddf2e5
msgid "What air data rate you choose will depend on the following factors:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-3dr-radio-advanced-configuration-and-technical-information.rst:155
#: eea15d7cc24b4e2fac094d4860ea30b7
msgid "What range you need"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-3dr-radio-advanced-configuration-and-technical-information.rst:156
#: 6fed1f3f25424277a0f2f5c7735f9db8
msgid "What data rate you will be sending"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-3dr-radio-advanced-configuration-and-technical-information.rst:157
#: 06b8580eb58b441783a3a123ab0747a7
msgid "Whether you primarily send in one direction, or both"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-3dr-radio-advanced-configuration-and-technical-information.rst:158
#: 30271fe05a5f44018cfd39a131008120
msgid "Whether you have ECC enabled"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-3dr-radio-advanced-configuration-and-technical-information.rst:160
#: f814bad1c38b45de966867d38ece7876
msgid "For most telemetry applications you will primarily be sending data mostly in one direction, from the aircraft to the ground station. For most people, the amount of data sent from the ground station to the aircraft is small, just an occasional control packet plus heartbeat packets."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-3dr-radio-advanced-configuration-and-technical-information.rst:166
#: 91ca5be2e72c4df2924fbb4ad351ad46
msgid "If you are using a joystick to control your aircraft then you will be sending a lot more data from the ground station to the aircraft, and in that case you may find a higher ``AIR_SPEED`` is needed, although your range will be reduced."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-3dr-radio-advanced-configuration-and-technical-information.rst:171
#: 3faed44b03874a7c823ce0bdad841649
msgid "The ``ECC`` parameter makes a big difference to the data rate you can support at a given ``AIR_SPEED``. If you have ECC set to zero, then no error correcting information is sent, and the radio uses a simple 16 bit CRC to detect transmission errors. In that case your radio will be able to support data transfers in one direction of around 90% of the ``AIR_SPEED``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-3dr-radio-advanced-configuration-and-technical-information.rst:178
#: 3b54cbc672d84fe08cdff8824c2aee28
msgid "If you enable ECC, then the data rate you can support is halved. The ECC system doubles the size of the data sent by the radios. It is worth it however, as the bit error rate will drop dramatically, and you are likely to get a much more reliable link at longer ranges."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-3dr-radio-advanced-configuration-and-technical-information.rst:184
#: 88d6b31de33a46e989f8b38f469554f8
msgid "ArduPilot will automatically adapt its telemetry rates to what the radio can handle, by using MAVLink RADIO packets injected into the MAVLink streams by the radio's firmware. That allows you to 'oversubscribe' your link, by setting up a ``SERIAL_SPEED`` larger than what the radios can actually handle."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-3dr-radio-advanced-configuration-and-technical-information.rst:190
#: 3c3a741350dc4464937e17162ca08fc2
msgid "The other factor in choosing the air data rate is the TDM 'sync time'. The two radios need to work out each others frequency hopping pattern. They do this by slowly changing the receive channel while rapidly changing the transmit channel. This process of getting in sync with the other radio takes just a few seconds at high air data rates, but gets slower for low air data rates."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-3dr-radio-advanced-configuration-and-technical-information.rst:197
#: 9e6167710d444ce19953f5cea6e89cdc
msgid "For most amateur UAV applications the default ``AIR_SPEED`` of 64 with no ECC enabled will be good."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-3dr-radio-advanced-configuration-and-technical-information.rst:201
#: 35a544af87ed408e845353d83d0b44de
msgid "Error correction"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-3dr-radio-advanced-configuration-and-technical-information.rst:203
#: d2caeab1df574f8aa53360c2268e5efe
msgid "Using error correction is no longer recommended due to the range reduction and the fact that some newer radio chips are not capable of doing ECC and will fail to function if this option is selected."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-3dr-radio-advanced-configuration-and-technical-information.rst:205
#: a685bf04a4d54652b9c97f221e55db3c
msgid "As mentioned above, the radios support a 12/24 Golay error correcting code if you set the ECC parameter to 1. This means that for every 12 bits of data the radio will send 24 bits, calculating the bits using Golay code lookup tables. The process is reversed on the receiving end, and allows the radio to correct bit errors of up to 3 bits in every 12 bits send (i.e. 25% bit error rate)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-3dr-radio-advanced-configuration-and-technical-information.rst:212
#: 3ff41965913f43ecb6ecbbd08aca7f02
msgid "The downside of the ECC option is that it halves your available data bandwidth.  In some cases this is worth it, as you are able to sustain a reliable link over longer ranges. You will also get a lot less 'noise' in the serial stream."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-3dr-radio-advanced-configuration-and-technical-information.rst:218
#: 5336ea84b6464e6fa922a694a9f8b05a
msgid "MAVLink framing"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-3dr-radio-advanced-configuration-and-technical-information.rst:220
#: f9251660cf3843bfac76cbb94f7ce9a3
msgid "If you set the MAVLINK option to 1 or 2 then the radio will do 'MAVLink framing'. The MAVLink protocol is used by ArduPilot for transmitting telemetry date to a ground station. When MAVLink framing is used, the radio will try to align radio packets with MAVLink packet boundaries. This means that if a packet is lost you don't end up with half a MAVLink packet being seen by the receiver. That partial packet would appear as line noise on your ground stations console."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-3dr-radio-advanced-configuration-and-technical-information.rst:228
#: affd683bf1454087b79e207bd05eca5c
msgid "If you set MAVLINK to 2, then in addition to doing MAVLink framing the radio will look for ``RC_OVERRIDE`` packets (used for joysticks) and ensure that those packets get sent as quickly as possible. This option is useful if you are using a tablet based joystick for control."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-3dr-radio-advanced-configuration-and-technical-information.rst:233
#: a1e8601677574929a9f534200b1ed58d
msgid "The radio firmware will try to fit multiple MAVLink packets into one radio packet where possible for maximum efficiency. The highest radio packet size is 252 bytes."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-3dr-radio-advanced-configuration-and-technical-information.rst:237
#: 6281f624c1e7450fa314d94c7f3ba969
msgid "The radio firmware supports both the MAVLink 1.0 and the MAVLink 2.0 transmission formats."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-3dr-radio-advanced-configuration-and-technical-information.rst:241
#: ae1af456acef4bbda95c065634484ed2
msgid "MAVLink reporting"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-3dr-radio-advanced-configuration-and-technical-information.rst:243
#: 1a478cdf48b745aca0547094f3d93bd4
msgid "If you have MAVLINK set to 1, then the radio firmware will also look for MAVLink HEARTBEAT messages coming from the serial connection. If it sees a HEARTBEAT message then it knows that the MAVLink protocol is in use, and it will start injecting MAVLink 'RADIO' status packets into the the serial stream."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-3dr-radio-advanced-configuration-and-technical-information.rst:249
#: 7710b7ea90454e00ae98a4a75fe9b8fe
msgid "The RADIO packets contain information about the RSSI (Received Signal Strength Indicator) level at both ends of the link, allowing the ground station or aircraft to take action in case the link quality falls too low."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-3dr-radio-advanced-configuration-and-technical-information.rst:254
#: 9ea6d8cfa27641bd9948428b92fc6110
msgid "The RADIO packets also contain information about error rates, and how full the serial transmit buffer is (as a percentage). ArduPilot can use this information to automatically adapt the telemetry stream rates to the data rate that the radios can sustain."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-3dr-radio-advanced-configuration-and-technical-information.rst:260
#: b4dcad1465a1480cb27bd4e6b7200791
msgid "Power levels"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-3dr-radio-advanced-configuration-and-technical-information.rst:262
#: 48b4d471f99c4d0dafcecc1239656896
msgid "You need to be very careful to configure your radios to stay within the legal power limits of the country you are operating in. The default power level of 20dBm is fine for the US and Australia, as up to 30dBm is allowed by the LIPD class licenses there in the 915-928MHz frequency band for a frequency hopping radio. So as long as your antennas have a gain of less than 10dBi you should be within the ISM rules."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-3dr-radio-advanced-configuration-and-technical-information.rst:269
#: a2b7ffc1c55a4433925a84461ce4ec9c
msgid "The radio cannot support arbitrary power levels. It can only support the power levels given in the following table"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-3dr-radio-advanced-configuration-and-technical-information.rst:315
#: c4d4d22aba5f44d4bb6be57c15ea9d3b
msgid "If you choose an unsupported power level the radio will choose the next highest power level from the above table."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-3dr-radio-advanced-configuration-and-technical-information.rst:318
#: b9f30cd813644bdfabf306370bcf4748
msgid "Please carefully check the EIRP (Equivalent isotropically radiated power) power limits for your country, making sure you take into account the antenna gain. The radio is a 'DIY' radio part and it is entirely your responsibility to ensure any use of it is compliant with local rules."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-3dr-radio-advanced-configuration-and-technical-information.rst:324
#: 917f84910e764d99a18c92d9460c9fa7
msgid "For example, if your local rules allow for a maximum of 30dBm (1W) EIRP, then if you use an amplifier with a 12dB transmit gain, and an antenna with 3dBi gain, then you will need to set TXPOWER to at most 14."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-3dr-radio-advanced-configuration-and-technical-information.rst:328
#: 8d31245f21124fdba5475f1d8ebd1c33
msgid "If you don't know how to calculate it, we've made a tutorial for you here: :ref:`Understanding dB, Watts and dBm <common-antenna-design_understanding_db_watts_and_dbm>`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-3dr-radio-advanced-configuration-and-technical-information.rst:332
#: 36d3f1bb328d4b0982ab553787c7063a
msgid "Using the AT command set"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-3dr-radio-advanced-configuration-and-technical-information.rst:334
#: 93f85645789e4c70804e5819278a0e50
msgid "The radios support a variant of the Hayes 'AT' modem command set for configuration."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-3dr-radio-advanced-configuration-and-technical-information.rst:337
#: f6f8eb1fa41c4607910aa23784cb781a
msgid "If you connect with a serial console to a radio at the current serial baud rate, you can tell the radio to enter AT command mode by entering the sequence '+++'. To prevent data being seen as the command sequence there is a guard time required, so make sure you type nothing on the serial link for 1 second before and after you enter the sequence."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-3dr-radio-advanced-configuration-and-technical-information.rst:343
#: b7f69f4666314334bc75c8af82d8cd84
msgid "When you enter AT command mode you will receive a 'OK' prompt from the radio and it will stop displaying data sent from the other radio."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-3dr-radio-advanced-configuration-and-technical-information.rst:346
#: 4c49ede97051465ab4693b3f68fa7a48
msgid "Once in AT command mode, you can give the radio either 'AT' commands to control the local radio, or (if successfully connected) you can use 'RT' commands to control the remote radio."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-3dr-radio-advanced-configuration-and-technical-information.rst:350
#: 63c28a23e8aa461fb92c0b42e5b6cb9f
msgid "The AT commands available are:"
msgstr ""

